<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hafez</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1325–1390</span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as Hafiz of Shiraz, Khwaja Shams al-Din Muhammad Hafez</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hafez was a Persian poet of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiraz">Shiraz</a>, the most celebrated master of the ghazal, or lyric love poem. He lived under the Injuid and Muzaffarid rulers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fars">Fars</a>, praising some patrons and mocking the piety of others. His collected poems, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/divan">Divan</a>, mix love, wine and mysticism, are still widely read and recited in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, and are used for divination.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hafez.jpg" alt="Hafez" width="320" height="376" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Anselm Feuerbach · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Anselm_Feuerbach_Hafis_vor_der_Schenke_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
